<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="a10-unit-007-excerpt"><section id="fs-id1170654953465">
<title>Simplify Expressions Using the Order of Operations</title>
<para id="fs-id1170655225397">To <term id="term-00015">simplify an expression</term> means to do all the math possible. For example, to simplify <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> we’d first multiply <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to get 8 and then add the 1 to get 9. A good habit to develop is to work down the page, writing each step of the process below the previous step. The example just described would look like this:</para>
<equation id="fs-id1170655083428" class="unnumbered"><label /><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></equation>
<para id="fs-id1170654905788">By not using an <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00016">equal sign</term> when you simplify an expression, you may avoid confusing expressions with equations.</para>
<note id="fs-id1170654905792">
<title>Simplify an Expression</title>
<para id="fs-id1170655107395">To <emphasis effect="bold">simplify an expression</emphasis>, do all operations in the expression.</para>
</note>
<para id="fs-id1170655351594">We’ve introduced most of the symbols and notation used in algebra, but now we need to clarify the order of operations. Otherwise, expressions may have different meanings, and they may result in different values. For example, consider the expression:</para>
<equation id="fs-id1170655125718" class="unnumbered"><label /><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></equation>
<para id="fs-id1170654942450">If you simplify this expression, what do you get?</para>
<para id="fs-id1170654942453">Some students say 49,</para>
<equation id="fs-id1170655178023" class="unnumbered"><label /><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mspace width="0.8em" /><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext>Since</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext>gives</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mspace width="0.8em" /><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext>And</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext>is</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>49</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mspace width="0.8em" /><m:mn>49</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></equation>
<para id="fs-id1170654906230">Others say 25,</para>
<equation id="fs-id1170654906233" class="unnumbered"><label /><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext>Since</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext>is</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>21</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>21</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext>And</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>21</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext>makes</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="center"><m:mn>25</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></equation>
<para id="fs-id1170655150670">Imagine the confusion in our banking system if every problem had several different correct answers!</para>
<para id="fs-id1170655150674">The same expression should give the same result. So mathematicians early on established some guidelines that are called the <term class="no-emphasis" id="term-00017">Order of Operations</term>.</para>
<note id="fs-id1170654985585" class="howto">
<title>Perform the Order of Operations.</title>
<list id="fs-id1166422648602" list-type="enumerated" number-style="arabic" class="stepwise">
<item>Parentheses and Other Grouping Symbols
<list id="fs-id1170655082463" list-type="bulleted" bullet-style="bullet">
<item>Simplify all expressions inside the parentheses or other grouping symbols, working on the innermost parentheses first.</item>
</list></item>
<item>Exponents
<list id="fs-id1170654914692" list-type="bulleted" bullet-style="bullet">
<item>Simplify all expressions with exponents.</item>
</list></item>
<item>Multiplication and Division
<list id="fs-id1170655178958" list-type="bulleted" bullet-style="bullet">
<item>Perform all multiplication and division in order from left to right. These operations have equal priority.</item>
</list></item>
<item>Addition and <emphasis effect="bold">S</emphasis>ubtraction
<list id="fs-id1170654943796" list-type="bulleted" bullet-style="bullet">
<item>Perform all addition and subtraction in order from left to right. These operations have equal priority.</item>
</list></item>
</list>
</note>
<note id="fs-id1166425080552" class="manipulative-math">Doing the Manipulative Mathematics activity “Game of 24” give you practice using the order of operations.</note>
<para id="fs-id1170654938531">Students often ask, “How will I remember the order?” Here is a way to help you remember: Take the first letter of each key word and substitute the silly phrase: “Please Excuse My Dear Aunt Sally.”</para>
<equation id="fs-id1170654007218" class="unnumbered"><label /><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">P</m:mtext><m:mtext>arentheses</m:mtext></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">P</m:mtext><m:mtext>lease</m:mtext></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">E</m:mtext><m:mtext>xponents</m:mtext></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">E</m:mtext><m:mtext>xcuse</m:mtext></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">M</m:mtext><m:mtext>ultiplication</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">D</m:mtext><m:mtext>ivision</m:mtext></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">M</m:mtext><m:mtext>y</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">D</m:mtext><m:mtext>ear</m:mtext></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">A</m:mtext><m:mtext>ddition</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">S</m:mtext><m:mtext>ubtraction</m:mtext></m:mtd><m:mtd /><m:mtd /><m:mtd columnalign="left"><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">A</m:mtext><m:mtext>unt</m:mtext><m:mspace width="0.2em" /><m:mtext mathvariant="bold">S</m:mtext><m:mtext>ally</m:mtext></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></equation>
<para id="fs-id1170654932101">It’s good that “<emphasis effect="bold">M</emphasis>y <emphasis effect="bold">D</emphasis>ear” goes together, as this reminds us that <emphasis effect="bold">m</emphasis>ultiplication and <emphasis effect="bold">d</emphasis>ivision have equal priority. We do not always do multiplication before division or always do division before multiplication. We do them in order from left to right.</para>
<para id="fs-id1170654914381">Similarly, “<emphasis effect="bold">A</emphasis>unt <emphasis effect="bold">S</emphasis>ally” goes together and so reminds us that <emphasis effect="bold">a</emphasis>ddition and <emphasis effect="bold">s</emphasis>ubtraction also have equal priority and we do them in order from left to right.</para>
<para id="fs-id1170654928166">Let’s try an example.</para>
<example id="fs-id1170654928169">
<exercise id="fs-id1170654954598">
<problem id="fs-id1170654954600">
<para id="fs-id1170654954602">Simplify: <span class="token">ⓐ</span> <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <span class="token">ⓑ</span> <m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170654939340">
<title>Solution</title>
<para id="fs-id1167836283034"><span class="token">ⓐ</span> <newline />
<table id="fs-id1167833022118" class="unnumbered unstyled can-break" aria-label="This figure has two columns, with text on the left and math on the right. On the first line in the right column is the expression 4 plus 3 times 7. One line down on the left, the text says: “Are there any parantheses? No. Are there any exponents? No. Is there any multiplication or division? Yes.” The first letters of “parantheses”, “exponents”, “multiplication” and “division” are bolded in reference to the first four letters in PEMDAS, or the order of operations. One line down, the text on the left says “Multiply first.” On the right is the expression 4 plus 3 times 7 again, with 3 times 7 written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Add.” On the right is the expression 4 plus 21. Beneath this expression is 25, the sum of 4 and 21."><label /><tgroup cols="2">
 <tbody>
  <row>
    <entry />
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836282522" alt="A mathematical expression displays the equation 4+3•7 in black text on a white background."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_005a_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Are there any <emphasis effect="bold">p</emphasis>arentheses? No.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Are there any <emphasis effect="bold">e</emphasis>xponents? No.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Is there any <emphasis effect="bold">m</emphasis>ultiplication or <emphasis effect="bold">d</emphasis>ivision? Yes.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Multiply first.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836477496" alt="A mathematical expression '4+3•7' is displayed on a white background. The number 4 and the plus sign are black, while the number 3, the multiplication dot, and the number 7 are red."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_005b_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Add.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167833397265" alt="The image displays a simple mathematical equation, '4+21' in a black font against a white background."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_005c_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry />
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836477509" alt="The number 25 is displayed in a dark gray font against a plain white background."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_005d_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>
</para>
<para id="fs-id1167836522115"><span class="token">ⓑ</span> <newline />
<table id="fs-id1167829713618" class="unnumbered unstyled" aria-label="This figure has two columns, with text on the left and math on the right. On the first line in the right column is the expression open parenthesis, 4 plus 3, close parenthesis, times 7. The open parenthesis, 4 plus 3, close parenthesis is written in red. One line down on the left, the text says: “Are there any parantheses? Yes.” The p in “parenthesis” is bolded in reference to the first letter of PEMDAS. One line down, the text on the left continues “Simplify inside the parentheses.”. On the right is the expression open parenthesis, 7, close parenthesis, 7, with the 7 in the parentheses written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Are there any exponents? No. Is there any multiplication or division? Yes.” The first letters of “exponents”, “multiplication” and “division” are bolded in reference to the second, third and fourth letters of PEMDAS.” On the last line in the figure, the text on the left says “Multiply.” On the right is the number 49."><label /><tgroup cols="2">
 <tbody>
  <row>
    <entry />
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836700561" alt="A mathematical expression shows the sum of 4 and 3, enclosed in parentheses, multiplied by 7. The expression is written as (4 + 3) ', 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_006a_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Are there any <emphasis effect="bold">p</emphasis>arentheses? Yes.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167824617198" alt="A mathematical expression showing (4+3) multiplied by 7. The numbers 4 and 3 are in red, while the plus sign, parentheses, multiplication dot, and the number 7 are in black or dark gray."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_006b_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Simplify inside the parentheses.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836329648" alt="The number 7 is shown twice, with the first 7 enclosed in parentheses and colored red, followed by a black 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_006c_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Are there any <emphasis effect="bold">e</emphasis>xponents? No.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Is there any <emphasis effect="bold">m</emphasis>ultiplication or <emphasis effect="bold">d</emphasis>ivision? Yes.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Multiply.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167829808043" alt="The number '49' is prominently displayed against a plain white background, rendered in a dark gray or black font with a slight blur effect, suggesting it might be part of a larger digital display or a simple graphic."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_006d_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>
</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</example>
<note id="fs-id1170654944310" class="try">
<exercise id="fs-id1170654936731">
<problem id="fs-id1170654936733">
<para id="fs-id1170654936735">Simplify: <span class="token">ⓐ</span> <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <span class="token">ⓑ</span> <m:math><m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170654942949">
<para id="fs-id1170654942951"><span class="token">ⓐ</span> 2 <span class="token">ⓑ</span> 14</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</note>
<note id="fs-id1170654941665" class="try">
<exercise id="fs-id1170655164725">
<problem id="fs-id1170655164727">
<para id="fs-id1170655164730">Simplify: <span class="token">ⓐ</span> <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <span class="token">ⓑ</span> <m:math><m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>·</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170655213403">
<para id="fs-id1170654966589"><span class="token">ⓐ</span> 35 <span class="token">ⓑ</span> 99</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</note>
<example id="fs-id1170654942986">
<exercise id="fs-id1170654942988">
<problem id="fs-id1170654942990">
<para id="fs-id1170654944773">Simplify: <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mo>÷</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170655114295">
<title>Solution</title>
<table id="fs-id1167836375645" class="unnumbered unstyled can-break" aria-label="This figure has two columns, with text on the left and math on the right. On the first line in the right column is the expression 18 divided by 6 plus 4, open parenthesis, 5 minus 2, close parenthesis. The open parenthesis, 5 minus 2, close parenthesis is written in red. One line down on the left, the text says: “Parentheses? Yes, subtract first.” On the right is the expression 18 divided by 6 plus four, open parenthesis, 3, close parenthesis. One line down, the text on the left continues “Exponents? No. Multiplication or division? Yes. Divide first because we multiply and divide left to right.” On the right is the expression 3 plus 4, open parenthesis, 3, close parenthesis. The 4 and the 3 in parentheses are written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Any other multiplication or division? Yes. Multiply.” On the right is the expression 3 plus 12 written in red. One line down on the left, the text says “Any other multiplication or division? No. Any addition or subtraction? Yes.” On the right is the number 15, which is the simplified expression."><label /><tgroup cols="2">
 <tbody>
  <row>
    <entry align="left" valign="top">Parentheses? Yes, subtract first.</entry>
    <entry><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mo>÷</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mrow></m:math><newline /><m:math><m:mrow><m:mspace width="0.2em" /></m:mrow></m:math><media id="fs-id1167836598935" alt="A mathematical expression showing 18 divided by 6, plus 4 multiplied by 3, which is 18 ÷ 6 + 4(3)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_007b_img.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Exponents? No.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Multiplication or division? Yes.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1171791418206" alt="A mathematical expression 18 ÷ 6 + 4(3) is displayed in red font on a white background, demonstrating the order of operations in arithmetic."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_007f_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Divide first because we multiply and divide left to right.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167833349691" alt="A mathematical expression '3 + 4(3)' is displayed, where the '4(3)' part is highlighted in red, indicating a multiplication operation to be performed before addition according to order of operations."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_007c_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Any other multiplication or division? Yes.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Multiply.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836622811" alt="A simple mathematical equation is displayed, showing '3 + 12' in a clear, dark font against a white background, representing an addition problem."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_007d_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Any other multiplication or division? No.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Any addition or subtraction? Yes.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167829596718" alt="The number '15' is displayed in dark gray on a white background."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_007e_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>
</solution>
</exercise>
</example>
<note id="fs-id1170655128975" class="try">
<exercise id="fs-id1170655117790">
<problem id="fs-id1170655117792">
<para id="fs-id1170655117794">Simplify: <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo>÷</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170654943705">
<para id="fs-id1170654936134">16</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</note>
<note id="fs-id1170654928200" class="try">
<exercise id="fs-id1170654928204">
<problem id="fs-id1170655232374">
<para id="fs-id1170655232376">Simplify: <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>70</m:mn><m:mo>÷</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170655288151">
<para id="fs-id1170655288153">23</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</note>
<para id="fs-id1170654928809">When there are multiple grouping symbols, we simplify the innermost parentheses first and work outward.</para>
<example id="fs-id1170655174684">
<exercise id="fs-id1170655174686">
<problem id="fs-id1170655228886">
<para id="fs-id1170655228888">Simplify: <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>[</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mrow><m:mo>]</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170654914329">
<title>Solution</title>
<table id="fs-id1167829840981" class="unnumbered unstyled can-break" aria-label="This figure has two columns, with text on the left and math on the right. On the first line in the right column is the expression 5 plus 2 cubed plus 3, open bracket, 6 minus 3, open parenthesis, 4 minus 2, close parenthesis, close bracket. One line down on the left, the text says: “Are there any parentheses (or other grouping symbol)? Yes. Focus on the parentheses that are inside the brackets.” On the right is the same expression repeated: 5 plus 2 cubed plus 3, open bracket, 6 minus 3, open parenthesis, 4 minus 2, close parenthesis, close bracket, with the 4 minus 2 in parantheses written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Subtract first.” On the right is the expression 5 plus 2 cubed plus 3, open bracket, 6 minus 3, open parenthesis, 2, close parenthesis, close bracket. The 2 in parentheses is written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Continue inside the brackets and multiply.” On the right is the expression 5 plus 2 cubed plus 3, open bracket, 6 minus 6, close bracket. The 6 minus 6 in brackets is written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Continue inside the brackets and subtract.” On the right is the expression 5 plus 2 cubed plus 3, open bracket, 0, close bracket. The 0 in brackets is written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “The expression inside the brackets requires no further simplification. Are there any exponents? Yes. Simplify exponents.” On the right is the same expression repeated again, 5 plus 2 cubed plus 3, open bracket, 0, close bracket. The 2 cubed is written in red. One line down, the text on the left says “Is there any multiplication or division? Yes. Multiply.” On the right is the expression 5 plus 8 plus 3, open bracket, 0, close bracket. The 0 in brackets is written in red. One line down on the left, the text says “Is there any addition or subtraction? Yes. Add.” On the right is the expression 5 plus 8 plus 0, with the 5 plus 8 written in red. One line down on the left, the text says “Add.” On the right is the expression 13 plus 0 written in red. Below that is the number 13, which is the simplified expression."><label /><tgroup cols="2">
 <tbody>
  <row>
    <entry />
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836287043" alt="A mathematical expression is displayed, featuring numbers and operations including addition, subtraction, exponentiation, and multiplication, organized with parentheses and brackets: 5 + 2^3 + 3[6 - 3(4 - 2)]."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008a_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Are there any parentheses (or other grouping symbol)? Yes.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Focus on the parentheses that are inside the brackets.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836326514" alt="A mathematical problem displaying an arithmetic expression: 5 + 2^3 + 3[6 - 3(4 - 2)]. The subtraction within the innermost parentheses, '4 - 2', is highlighted in red."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008b_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Subtract.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836578693" alt="A mathematical expression reads 5 + 2^3 + 3[6 - 3(2)]. The '3(2)' part is highlighted in red, indicating a specific focus or step in solving the problem."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008c_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Continue inside the brackets and multiply.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167833142573" alt="A mathematical expression displays 5 + 2 cubed + 3 multiplied by the quantity 6 minus 6, with the second 6 highlighted in red."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008d_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Continue inside the brackets and subtract.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167824720931" alt="A mathematical expression showing '5 + 2^3 + 3[0]'. The number 0 is highlighted in red within the brackets, indicating a specific element or value."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008e_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>The expression inside the brackets requires no further simplification.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Are there any exponents? Yes.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836571320" alt="A mathematical expression showing 5 plus 2 raised to the power of 3, plus 3 multiplied by 0."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008f_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Simplify exponents.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836363366" alt="A mathematical expression '5 + 8 + 3[0]' is displayed on a white background, with the '3[0]' part highlighted in red text, suggesting a specific focus on that segment of the equation."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008g_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Is there any multiplication or division? Yes.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Multiply.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836628989" alt="An image showing the mathematical expression 5+8+0. The numbers '5' and '8' along with the first plus sign appear in red, while the second plus sign and the number '0' are black."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008h_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Is there any addition or subtraction? Yes.</entry>
    <entry />
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Add.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836408301" alt="The mathematical expression '13 + 0' is displayed in a red, slightly blurred font against a white background."><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008i_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>Add.</entry>
    <entry><media id="fs-id1167836321064" alt="13"><image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_ElemAlg_Figure_01_02_008j_img_new.jpg" /></media></entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>
</solution>
</exercise>
</example>
<note id="fs-id1170655222643" class="try">
<exercise id="fs-id1170655222647">
<problem id="fs-id1170654924513">
<para id="fs-id1170654924515">Simplify: <m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>[</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mrow><m:mo>]</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170655201328">
<para id="fs-id1170655201331">86</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</note>
<note id="fs-id1170654936028" class="try">
<exercise id="fs-id1170654942628">
<problem id="fs-id1170654942630">
<para id="fs-id1170654942632">Simplify: <m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>[</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mrow><m:mo>]</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
</problem>
<solution id="fs-id1170655163264">
<para id="fs-id1170654940244">1</para>
</solution>
</exercise>
</note>
</section>
